      "text": "To take one instance, speakers or writers may well understand themselves as working with conscious intent, yet the intention may be causally irrelevant to the effects produced in the audience. Rhetorical theory tends to assume that intent equals result: a rhetor wants to persuade a group of people to vote a certain way, the rhetor succeeds, and therefore the rhetor's intent is held to have been successfully realized through his or her rhetorical art. Arabella Lyon remarks that rhetoric has maintained its boundaries by this adherence to discourse understood as instrumental (4). This is also rhetoric's built-in retroactivity, according to which what works is always telegraphed by an initial intentional action. Curiously, however, intent is rarely called into question when a rhetorical message goes awry; rather, the issue becomes a matter of technique. This idea underpins the longstanding idea of rhetoric as a teachable art. Failure results because one's message was not sufficient for the intent, because one was unprepared for the pa rticular audience, because one made a mistake, and so on. There is no leeway for accidental persuasion, for persuasion at odds with or in spite of intent or even the artistry of rhetorical work. Instead, the causality of rhetorical intent actualized through rhetorical work is often assumed as an unquestionable given. A great deal of excellent work, however, has been done on aleatory methods (see Vitanza, \"From Heuristic\"); unconscious forms of persuasion, such as Burke's identification, taken from psychoanalysis (see D. Davis, Inessential; Lundberg and Gunn); complexity and ecology (Cooper, Dobrin, Edbauer, Hawk, Lanham); and various proponents of hermeneutic and postmodern thought (Biesecker; Hyde; Lyon; Mailloux, \"Rhetorical Hermeneutics\" and \"Rhetorical Hermeneutics Still\"). It is\n\nthus perhaps surprising that we have not made more progress here. But as Lyon suggests, intent has in fact been woefully undertheorized in rhetoric precisely because it is always assumed (3-4). What I have described as essential to an ambient rhetoric-its embodied and embedded or situated character, its dispersal across things that themselves have gradations of agency, and its dynamic emergence within an environment that occasions certain effects-suggests that intent is only one element in a large array of things, feelings, peoples, and forces all complexly interacting. To say this is not to deny the existence or importance of intent but rather to insist that within any given rhetorical event, intent cannot suffice for its full accounting as rhetoric-not to mention the near dogmatic assertion of intent as the expression of the subjective will.\n\nThe emphasis on self-consciousness, another aspect of Cole's definition, is just as problematic. Each of the four strands of thought I brought to the notion of ambient rhetoric emphasizes the idea that at any given time, we are only partially conscious of what we are doing, why we are doing it, and what will result from it. When we tether intent to self-consciousness, we cut off large swaths of human activity from rhetorical practice in our rhetorical theory. That last prepositional phrase is crucial: the point is not that what is denied to self-consciousness fails to come into play in rhetorical engagement but that we deny inquiry into those aspects when our rhetorical theory opens itself only onto what is immediately self-conscious and thus easily tethered to humanist notions of willing. Intent and selfconsciousness no doubt matter enormously, but they no longer suffice to determine what rhetoric is and is not. Behind the conventional notion of intention lies a model of human being as an autonomous, self-knowing, subjective agent. Everything I have here described works to undo and disperse this model as a reduced view of human being.\n\nIn sum, I hope this book helps us to see such narrowing as a way to impoverish rhetoric when we should be opening it up, not to make it bigger, but to attune us, in rhetoric and via rhetoric, to its fullest flowering. Rhetoric's timeliness as a field of inquiry and a practice has never been more propitious. As I suggested previously, transformations in ourselves, in our historical conjuncture, and in our built environments have occasioned revised understandings of human being and the world. The dynamically ecological imbrications of places, things, and informatics can be read as a (partial) rhetoricization of the world in the most concrete and material way imaginable. Inhere is a need for an ambient rhetoric, it is because we have been called for it to be so, not as passive recipients of an impartial, inhuman",
