    {
      "raw_rank": 5,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00157",
      "distance": 0.850054919719696,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 264,
      "page_end": 264,
      "text": "In short, like everything else, dwelling resists any reduction to forms of simple presence, in this case, to directly available and revealed best practices for homemaking. If we were always already at home, if home were never at issue (never a \"thing\" for us), then homemaking would not be challenging for us; ultimately it can never be fully realized. Dwelling is an ongoing and never stilled process ofattunement, disclosure, and building. Thus, dwelling is rhetorical in an ambient sense: disclosure and attunement emerge out of a worldly affectability, so that dwelling's coming to manifestation is a matter of ongoing differentiation. In what follows, I will try to elaborate on this point more plainly; specifically, I will try to rethink stability in an ambient key, arguing that it is less a cessation of movement or change than a way of dwelling attuned to sustainability and achieved through the lived realization of sufficiency.\n\nDwelling in/as Sufficiency\n\nBringing Heidegger's concept of dwelling into ongoing struggles with sustainability, particularly regarding the automobile, does not just show but performs how humans become cognizant of and organize their practices around what in contemporary environmental discourse we would call an ecology, or things and forces joined together in dynamic coexistence sustainable over time. The English word ecology, which derives from the German Okologie, coined by the biologist Ernst Haeckel in 1866, stems from the ancient Greek word oikos, meaning \"house,\" \"home,\" or \"dwelling.\" Haeckel drew on oikos to bring home the sense of ecology as a comprehensive science of an organism's relation to its environment,\n\nwith environment here being where one dwells (Thiele.Indra's 3). As I have shown, when Heidegger discusses dwelling, he describes the relation not only as one of directly co-responding but as one of hearkening to directives or being conditioned by things; for Heidegger, that is, ecology entails more than presence. Ecological literature, however, often describes ecologies in (sometimes overly) holistic terms focused on the powerful role of the feedback loops that emerge within circulating, sustaining activities and material interactions. Feedback loops supply necessary encouragement and constraint allowing for the dynamic self-organization characteristic of a flourishing ecology; this can even be attributed to a direct sense of a \"natural\" law that we must acknowledge or follow.• Regulatory mechanisms and their \"values\" are in a sense \"built in\" to an ecological system. Thus ecologies are often characterized in terms of efficiency, since they tend to reward the maximization of potential. At the same time. ecologies curtail or otherwise redirect efficiency so that nothing gets too far out of balance; if it does, the ecology \"self-corrects,\" adapts, morphs, or fails in accordance with autopoesis, or self-organization (Maturana and Varela, 47-52; Varela, Thompson, and Rosch 88-91: Hayles 136). In short, while efficiency certainly constitutes a vector for an ecology, the notion relevant here can significantly differ from the kind of abstracted and totalized notion of efficiency common to instrumentalized technological thinking,5Efficiency matters because an ecology is at every moment entangled in constraining forces-checks and balances, if you will-necessary for a sustainable trajectory. Insofar as efficiency is thus always tempered, we might better consider it an example of sufficiency. In a sustainable ecological system, efficiency emerges as forms of sufficiency (Thiele, Timely 179).\n\nWhat is at stake in this qualification of efficiency? We might keep in mind the general definition of efficiency: maximum yield for minimum effort, a point that resonates with Heidegger's arguments about technological enframing insofar as both can be conceived as forms of ordering (QCT 8, 15)_ More precisely, technology already conditions how efficiency is conceived and I ived. This occurs not deterministically but rather ontologically. in shaping how worldly affordances reveal themselves as what they are, for technology is a way of revealing the world, or more pointedly, a revealing that challenges (herausfordern) the world as a standing reserve (Bestand), a stockpile of resources to be exploited (QCT 17). I explored this idea in previous chapters through discussions of Sputnik andEarthrise. In particular, Heidegger's claim that Sputnik is already spoken by hurry illuminates how efficiency orchestrates not a way of viewing the world so much as a way of",
